<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nartiang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nartiang is a village in the West Jaintia Hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meghalaya">Meghalaya</a>, north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, once a summer seat of the Jaintia kings. It is known for one of the largest groups of megaliths in the region: hundreds of standing stones and flat table stones raised as memorials by clans and rulers. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a> temple at Nartiang reflects the court's adoption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> worship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nartiang.jpg" alt="Nartiang" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oliver walsher · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nartiang_Durga_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
